<h1 class="article-title">Liepāja SEZ Authority Begins Removing Historical Pollution from the Karosta Canal</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>The Liepāja SEZ Authority continues implementing the project “Remediation of Historically Contaminated Sites in the Karosta Canal at the Port of Liepāja,” aiming to reduce the harmful impact of historically polluted sediments on the environment, human health and the ecological condition of the Baltic Sea. The remediation contractor is EKO OSTA Ltd, which submitted the most economically advantageous tender and was awarded the contract. The work involves dredging approximately 164,000 m³ of contaminated sediments at a total contract value of EUR 3,497,260. The project is financed under the Norwegian Financial Mechanism 2014–2021 programme “Climate Change Mitigation, Adaptation and Environment.”</p><p>The Karosta Canal remediation programme was approved in early April. EKO OSTA Ltd mobilised the necessary equipment and began removing contaminated sediment from the canal, with completion scheduled by 30 November 2023. The work will remove sediment that fails to meet legal standards and contains approximately 400 tonnes of hazardous chemical substances.</p><p>Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis explained that the Karosta Canal environmental remediation project differs significantly from the Authority’s other projects: while it also supports development, it must first deal with the Soviet military legacy. “Environmental and ecological issues matter greatly. That is why it is so important to remove sediment from the Karosta Canal bed, where almost the entire periodic table can be found, and eliminate this HELCOM Baltic Sea hotspot. It is a long-planned, unique project that will make a cleaner environment possible, end concerns about oil slicks in the Karosta Canal and allow active business development in the canal basin and surrounding areas.”</p><p>The Liepāja Special Economic Zone Authority warns that rainbow-coloured films of petroleum products may be visible on the Karosta Canal during the work. The contractor, Eko Osta Ltd, will collect these and ensure contaminated sediments are deposited only in the designated area.</p><p>The project will help achieve the objectives and actions of the Helsinki Commission (HELCOM) Baltic Sea Action Plan: restoring a good ecological condition in the Baltic Sea, with natural biodiversity and dynamics, clean, healthy and productive waters, and sustainable use of the marine environment for present and future generations. The results will reduce residents’ exposure to historical pollution and substantially improve marine ecology and the surrounding environment.</p><p>This project continues previous efforts to clean the Karosta Canal at the Port of Liepāja. With EU Cohesion Fund support, the first phase of the project, “Remediation of Historically Contaminated Sites in the Karosta Canal at the Port of Liepāja,” was completed by 2015. Large quantities of man-made debris, including scrap metal, concrete structures and wood, were removed and disposed of across 78 hectares. About 60,000 m³ of contaminated sediment was also removed from the most polluted ten-hectare area and deposited in a specially equipped facility at the eastern end of the canal.</p><p>Cleaning the Karosta Canal is a project of European significance, so its outcomes must follow best practice in environmental protection. One of the partners, the Norwegian Institute for Water Research (NIVA), will share experience from similar remediation projects in Norway and provide expertise to Latvian specialists responsible for maintaining the improvements over time. The work is implemented by the Liepāja SEZ Authority together with NIVA and the Baltic Coasts association.</p><p> </p><p>Working together for a green Europe!</p><p>The project is financed by the Norwegian Financial Mechanism 2014–2021 programme “Climate Change Mitigation, Adaptation and Environment.” Total project costs amount to EUR 5,117,647.05, including EUR 4,350,000 in Norwegian Financial Mechanism funding.</p></div>
